1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,267
You get paid.

2
00:00:01,287 --> 00:00:02,977
For about a week, you feel rich.

3
00:00:03,633 --> 00:00:05,384
Then, somehow, the money is gone.

4
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:07,569
And you don't even know where it went.

5
00:00:08,333 --> 00:00:09,623
So you promise yourself:

6
00:00:09,643 --> 00:00:11,064
next month, I'll save.

7
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:12,349
Next month comes.

8
00:00:12,369 --> 00:00:13,088
Same thing.

9
00:00:13,733 --> 00:00:15,722
And here's the part nobody tells you.

10
00:00:17,033 --> 00:00:18,848
It's not about how much you earn.

11
00:00:18,868 --> 00:00:20,458
And it's not about discipline.

12
00:00:21,167 --> 00:00:22,802
Your brain is built to spend now,

13
00:00:22,822 --> 00:00:24,077
and forget about later.

14
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,195
Psychologists call it present bias.

15
00:00:27,215 --> 00:00:28,647
A euro today feels bigger

16
00:00:28,667 --> 00:00:30,076
than a euro next year.

17
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:32,931
But researchers found something interesting.

18
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:35,852
People who save a lot don't have more willpower.

19
00:00:35,872 --> 00:00:37,064
They just set things up

20
00:00:37,084 --> 00:00:38,821
so saving happens without them.

21
00:00:39,633 --> 00:00:41,435
Today, I'll show you eight simple

22
00:00:41,455 --> 00:00:43,824
systems that make saving almost automatic.

23
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:45,808
No spreadsheets.

24
00:00:45,828 --> 00:00:46,058
No guilt.

25
00:00:46,078 --> 00:00:46,653
Let's begin.

26
00:00:47,767 --> 00:00:48,004
One.

27
00:00:48,024 --> 00:00:49,293
Pay yourself first.

28
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:51,705
Most people save like this:

29
00:00:51,725 --> 00:00:54,140
get paid, spend, and save whatever is left.

30
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:56,733
But there is almost never anything left.

31
00:00:57,467 --> 00:00:59,177
Here's what people who save do:

32
00:00:59,197 --> 00:01:01,748
get paid, save first, then spend what's left.

33
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:04,285
Set up an automatic transfer

34
00:01:04,305 --> 00:01:05,620
for the day after payday.

35
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:08,152
Even fifty euros is enough to start.

36
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:10,500
Because money you never see in your

37
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:12,480
current account is money you don't miss.

38
00:01:13,567 --> 00:01:13,797
Two.

39
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:15,283
Make saving the default.

40
00:01:16,133 --> 00:01:17,438
In one famous study,

41
00:01:17,458 --> 00:01:19,480
a company changed just one thing.

42
00:01:19,867 --> 00:01:23,242
New employees were signed up for the pension plan automatically,

43
00:01:23,262 --> 00:01:24,623
unless they said no.

44
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:29,632
Participation jumped from about 37% to 86%.

45
00:01:29,967 --> 00:01:30,847
Same company.

46
00:01:30,867 --> 00:01:31,702
Same plan.

47
00:01:31,722 --> 00:01:33,339
Only the default changed.

48
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,775
So make everything a default:

49
00:01:35,795 --> 00:01:38,803
automatic saving, automatic investing, and round-ups,

50
00:01:38,823 --> 00:01:40,380
if your bank offers them.

51
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:42,162
Every decision you remove is

52
00:01:42,182 --> 00:01:43,459
one you can't get wrong.

53
00:01:44,667 --> 00:01:44,952
Three.

54
00:01:44,972 --> 00:01:46,448
Make spending hurt a little.

55
00:01:47,333 --> 00:01:49,328
Handing over cash feels painful.

56
00:01:49,348 --> 00:01:50,372
Tapping a card or a

57
00:01:50,392 --> 00:01:52,384
phone barely feels like anything.

58
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:55,260
In one experiment at MIT,

59
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,892
people paying by card offered up to

60
00:01:56,912 --> 00:01:59,746
twice as much for the same basketball tickets.

61
00:02:00,567 --> 00:02:01,832
When money is invisible,

62
00:02:01,852 --> 00:02:03,624
your brain barely feels it leave.

63
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:06,888
So delete saved cards from shopping sites.

64
00:02:06,908 --> 00:02:08,688
Turn off one-click buying.

65
00:02:08,708 --> 00:02:10,912
Delete the apps you shop on when you're bored.

66
00:02:11,333 --> 00:02:13,552
A few seconds of friction is often enough.

67
00:02:14,733 --> 00:02:15,023
Four.

68
00:02:15,043 --> 00:02:16,705
The 72-hour rule.

69
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:19,850
When you want something you don't need,

70
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:20,100
don't say no.

71
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:20,655
Just say:

72
00:02:20,675 --> 00:02:21,129
not yet.

73
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:23,849
Put it in your basket, close the page,

74
00:02:23,869 --> 00:02:24,984
and wait three days.

75
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:27,590
Most of the time, the urge is gone.

76
00:02:27,610 --> 00:02:28,785
You didn't want the thing.

77
00:02:28,805 --> 00:02:29,847
You wanted the feeling.

78
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:32,915
If you still want it after three days,

79
00:02:32,935 --> 00:02:33,165
buy it.

80
00:02:33,185 --> 00:02:33,923
Without guilt.

81
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:35,427
Five.

82
00:02:35,447 --> 00:02:37,026
Give every euro a job.

83
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:39,655
Money sitting in one big account

84
00:02:39,675 --> 00:02:41,501
feels like it's all there to spend.

85
00:02:42,267 --> 00:02:43,647
So split it into pots:

86
00:02:43,667 --> 00:02:46,589
rent, food, fun, emergencies, and future you.

87
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:49,310
In one study in rural India,

88
00:02:49,330 --> 00:02:51,279
workers saved more when their savings were

89
00:02:51,299 --> 00:02:53,546
split into two envelopes instead of one.

90
00:02:53,867 --> 00:02:55,527
And even more when the envelopes had

91
00:02:55,547 --> 00:02:57,232
a picture of their children on them.

92
00:02:57,967 --> 00:02:59,380
Most banking apps now let

93
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:00,712
you create pots for free.

94
00:03:00,732 --> 00:03:01,985
Give each one a name.

95
00:03:03,133 --> 00:03:03,363
Six.

96
00:03:03,383 --> 00:03:05,905
Fix big things once, not small things daily.

97
00:03:06,767 --> 00:03:08,279
Skipping a coffee every day

98
00:03:08,299 --> 00:03:10,154
takes willpower every single day.

99
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,535
Your phone plan, your insurance, your bank fees,

100
00:03:13,555 --> 00:03:14,530
your subscriptions:

101
00:03:14,550 --> 00:03:15,610
fix them once,

102
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:17,437
and you save every month after that.

103
00:03:18,167 --> 00:03:21,267
Open your bank app, scroll through the last three months,

104
00:03:21,287 --> 00:03:22,888
and cancel anything you wouldn't

105
00:03:22,908 --> 00:03:24,237
sign up for again today.

106
00:03:25,033 --> 00:03:26,415
One boring hour can save you

107
00:03:26,435 --> 00:03:28,225
more than a year of skipped coffees.

108
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:29,791
Seven.

109
00:03:29,811 --> 00:03:30,881
Save your next raise,

110
00:03:30,901 --> 00:03:32,300
before you get used to it.

111
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:35,435
When people earn more, they usually spend more:

112
00:03:35,455 --> 00:03:38,148
a bigger flat, a nicer car, more dinners out.

113
00:03:38,467 --> 00:03:40,508
Economists call it lifestyle creep.

114
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:44,815
Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi tested a simple fix:

115
00:03:44,835 --> 00:03:47,557
commit today to save part of your future raises.

116
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,290
For the people who joined,

117
00:03:49,310 --> 00:03:52,269
savings rates went from 3.5%

118
00:03:52,289 --> 00:03:54,970
to 13.6% in forty months.

119
00:03:55,833 --> 00:03:57,373
So next time you get a raise,

120
00:03:57,393 --> 00:03:58,420
send half of it to

121
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:00,328
savings from the very first month.

122
00:04:00,348 --> 00:04:02,672
You won't miss money you never got used to.

123
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:04,260
Eight.

124
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,724
Meet your future self.

125
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:07,310
To your brain,

126
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:09,726
your future self feels almost like a stranger.

127
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:11,775
In one experiment,

128
00:04:11,795 --> 00:04:14,577
people who saw an aged version of their own face

129
00:04:14,597 --> 00:04:16,068
chose to set aside about

130
00:04:16,088 --> 00:04:17,868
twice as much for retirement.

131
00:04:18,667 --> 00:04:20,272
Your future self is real.

132
00:04:20,292 --> 00:04:22,556
They will live with every choice you make today.

133
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:27,005
So once you have a safety cushion of three to six months of expenses,

134
00:04:27,025 --> 00:04:28,296
put the rest to work.

135
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:32,520
A cheap, diversified index fund, every month, automatically.

136
00:04:33,133 --> 00:04:35,123
That's how saving turns into building.

137
00:04:36,433 --> 00:04:37,612
Final message.

138
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:41,065
Saving money isn't about being cheap.

139
00:04:41,085 --> 00:04:42,700
And it isn't about willpower.

140
00:04:43,367 --> 00:04:44,896
It's about building a system that

141
00:04:44,916 --> 00:04:46,196
does the hard part for you.

142
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:48,003
Pay yourself first.

143
00:04:48,023 --> 00:04:49,178
Make it automatic.

144
00:04:49,198 --> 00:04:50,458
Add a little friction.

145
00:04:50,478 --> 00:04:51,167
Wait three days.

146
00:04:51,187 --> 00:04:52,248
Name your pots.

147
00:04:52,268 --> 00:04:53,718
Fix the big things once.

148
00:04:53,738 --> 00:04:54,818
Save your raises.

149
00:04:54,838 --> 00:04:56,519
And remember who you're saving for.

150
00:04:57,333 --> 00:04:58,033
Set it up once,

151
00:04:58,053 --> 00:04:59,878
and it keeps working while you sleep.

152
00:05:00,633 --> 00:05:03,068
This is education, not financial advice.

153
00:05:03,088 --> 00:05:04,833
If this helped, subscribe.

154
00:05:04,853 --> 00:05:05,693
And next up:

155
00:05:05,713 --> 00:05:07,413
every ETF, explained.
